# Target C-II — compatible common FREE trunks have forced two-normal spatial drift

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; metric refinement of the parity-compatible common-trunk branch  
**Scope:** `COMMON-TRUNK` after the order/orientation parity split  
**Dependencies:** ordered two-normal shared skeleton; small-external common lower runs; common-run parity refinement  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_common_trunk_two_normal_cone_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The parity refinement leaves exactly two ways in which a multi-edge common trunk can survive: `SAME` traversal with `SAME-ORDER`, or `OPPOSITE` traversal with `REVERSE-ORDER`. The lower-gate first-hit identity then forces every edge of that trunk into an explicit cone determined only by the two module normals. This converts a large shared matching into genuine Euclidean displacement.

Let `P,Q` be the two all-lower paths, let `C` be one connected edge-containing component of `P\cap Q`, and orient every edge of `C` according to the traversal of `P`. Let `N_1,N_2` be the two unit module normals and write

\[
\theta:=\angle(N_1,N_2)\in(0,\pi).
\]

Every oriented lower-gate edge vector `r` in module `k` has a positive activation increment `Delta_k` and satisfies

\[
|r-\Delta_kN_k|=1<|r|.
\]

Therefore

\[
r\cdot N_k
={|r|^2-1+\Delta_k^2\over2\Delta_k}>0.
\tag{CT.1}
\]

The sign in the second module depends only on the common-trunk traversal parity.

## SAME + SAME-ORDER

Here both paths traverse `C` in the same direction. Thus every oriented trunk edge satisfies

\[
r\cdot N_1>0,\qquad r\cdot N_2>0.
\]

Let

\[
B:={N_1+N_2\over|N_1+N_2|}
\]

be the unit internal bisector. Elementary two-half-plane geometry gives

\[
\boxed{
 r\cdot B>|r|\sin(\theta/2)>\sin(\theta/2).
}
\tag{CT.2}
\]

Hence, if the common trunk has `L` edges and endpoint displacement `X`,

\[
\boxed{
X\cdot B>L\sin(\theta/2).
}
\tag{CT.3}
\]

If the trunk contains `s` edges of the selected vertex-disjoint matching, then no two selected edges are adjacent. Therefore

\[
L\ge2s-1,
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
X\cdot B>(2s-1)\sin(\theta/2).
}
\tag{CT.4}
\]

So a same-direction common trunk cannot fold back along the bisector: every one of its edges spends positive bisector displacement.

## OPPOSITE + REVERSE-ORDER

Here `Q` traverses the common component in the direction opposite to `P`. With `r` still oriented according to `P`, the second lower-gate condition is

\[
(-r)\cdot N_2>0,
\]

so

\[
r\cdot N_1>0,\qquad r\cdot N_2<0.
\]

Let

\[
T:={N_1-N_2\over|N_1-N_2|}
\]

be the unit difference direction. The corresponding cone geometry gives

\[
\boxed{
 r\cdot T>|r|\cos(\theta/2)>\cos(\theta/2).
}
\tag{CT.5}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
X\cdot T>L\cos(\theta/2)
\ge(2s-1)\cos(\theta/2).
}
\tag{CT.6}
\]

When additionally

\[
0<\theta\le\pi/2,
\]

the same geometry gives the individual-normal bound

\[
0<r\cdot N_1<|r|\sin\theta,
\qquad
0<(-r)\cdot N_2<|r|\sin\theta.
\tag{CT.7}
\]

The restriction `theta<=pi/2` is necessary for (CT.7): for obtuse normal separation the opposite-traversal cone contains the direction `N_1` (or `-N_2`), so an individual-normal projection can approach `|r|`. The unconditional bound is instead

\[
0<r\cdot N_1,
\quad
0<(-r)\cdot N_2,
\quad
\max\{r\cdot N_1,(-r)\cdot N_2\}
\le|r|\sin(\min\{\theta,\pi/2\}).
\tag{CT.7'}
\]

Thus an opposite-direction shared trunk is forced into the transverse cone between the two normal half-planes; the sharper `sin(theta)` individual-normal control is used only in the close-normal regime, where `theta=o(1)`.

---

## A tuning that makes every surviving common trunk spatially unbounded

The previous small-external theorem assumes

\[
Z_D:=|A\setminus F|=o(\sqrt D).
\]

Define

\[
R_D:={D^{1/4}\over\sqrt{Z_D+1}}\longrightarrow\infty
\tag{CT.8}
\]

and choose the admissible slow reflection scale

\[
\boxed{
\kappa_D:=D^{1/8}(Z_D+1)^{1/4}.
}
\tag{CT.9}
\]

Then

\[
\kappa_D=o(D^{1/4}),
\qquad
m=\Theta(D^{1/4}/\kappa_D)=\Theta(R_D^{1/2}).
\tag{CT.10}
\]

The common-lower-run theorem gives

\[
q=\Omega\!\left({\kappa_DD^{1/4}\over Z_D+1}\right)
=\Omega(R_D^{3/2})
\tag{CT.11}
\]

selected shared inverse-`D` gates on one fixed run pair. The balanced trunk/cycle-rank fork uses

\[
s=\sqrt q=\Omega(R_D^{3/4}).
\tag{CT.12}
\]

The two-normal extraction with its existing choice `omega_D=sqrt(m)` gives

\[
\theta=\Omega(m^{-1/2})=\Omega(R_D^{-1/4}).
\tag{CT.13}
\]

Consequently, if the balanced fork lands in a compatible common trunk, (CT.4)--(CT.6) give:

### SAME + SAME-ORDER trunk

\[
\boxed{
X\cdot B=\Omega(R_D^{1/2})\to\infty.
}
\tag{CT.14}
\]

### OPPOSITE + REVERSE-ORDER trunk

\[
\boxed{
X\cdot T=\Omega(R_D^{3/4})\to\infty.
}
\tag{CT.15}
\]

Thus the small-external common-trunk branch can be retuned so that a surviving trunk is not merely combinatorially long. Its endpoints separate by a diverging Euclidean amount in a canonically determined direction.

Combining this with the parity refinement yields the following residual normal form at the tuned scale:

1. `PARITY-MISMATCH`: `beta(P union Q)=Omega(R_D^(3/2))`;
2. parity-compatible `CYCLE-RANK`: `beta(P union Q)=Omega(R_D^(3/4))`;
3. `SAME/SAME` common trunk: bisector displacement `Omega(R_D^(1/2))`;
4. `OPPOSITE/REVERSE` common trunk: difference-axis displacement `Omega(R_D^(3/4))`.

The selected trunk gates retain their original metric bound

\[
1<|e|\le1+K/D
\]

and are exact lower gates in both repairs.

---

## Cone proof

Put the unit bisector `B` on angle zero and write the two normals at angles `+theta/2` and `-theta/2`.

For SAME traversal, an edge direction angle `phi` must satisfy

\[
\cos(\phi-\theta/2)>0,
\qquad
\cos(\phi+\theta/2)>0.
\]

Their common interval is

\[
|\phi|<\pi/2-\theta/2.
\]

Hence

\[
\cos\phi>\sin(\theta/2),
\]

which proves (CT.2).

For OPPOSITE traversal, choose the sign of `T` so that it points from `N_2` toward `N_1`. The conditions become

\[
\cos(\phi-\theta/2)>0,
\qquad
\cos(\phi+\theta/2)<0.
\]

Thus `phi` lies in the angular interval of width `theta` centered on `T`, so

\[
r\cdot T>|r|\cos(\theta/2).
\]

The difference-axis estimate proves (CT.5). For the individual-normal projection, the extremum over this interval is `|r| sin(theta)` when `theta<=pi/2`, but becomes `|r|` once `theta>=pi/2`; this proves the corrected (CT.7)--(CT.7').

C-II remains open. No theorem-state authority is changed.

**Disposition:** converted the parity-compatible common-trunk branch into a two-normal cone theorem and retuned the small-external slow scale so every surviving common trunk has diverging Euclidean displacement, while parity-mismatch retains the stronger full-`q` cycle-rank conclusion.
